---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import AlertBox from '../../components/ui/AlertBox.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'sample', label: 'Annotated example proposal' },
  { id: 'good', label: 'What a stronger proposal does' },
  { id: 'red-flags', label: 'Ambiguities to resolve' },
  { id: 'change-orders', label: 'Allowances & change orders' },
  { id: 'before-signing', label: 'Before signing' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title="What a Good Standby Generator Quote Looks Like: Annotated Example"
  description="A clearly fictional, educational standby-generator proposal showing the equipment, fuel, electrical, site-work, permit, commissioning, warranty, exclusions, allowance, and change-order details worth having in writing."
  category="Cost" categoryHref="/cost/" updated="2026-10-04" verified="2026-10-04" image="/images/cost-install-quote.webp" readingTime="9 min read" toc={toc}
  takeaways={[
    'A useful quote identifies exact equipment and project scope; it does not rely on labels like “22 kW package” or “whole house” alone.',
    'Allowances and exclusions are not automatically bad, but they should say what is unknown, who will verify it, and how the final price changes.',
    'This page is a fictional educational example—not a contractor bid, price benchmark, legal form, or recommended contract language.',
  ]}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Generac: backup generator installation process and consultation topics', href: 'https://www.generac.com/resources/home-management/backup-generator-installation/', type: 'Manufacturer', verified: '2026-10-04' },
    { label: 'Cummins: home generator FAQ — installation, permitting and professional-installation overview', href: 'https://www.cummins.com/en-na/na/generators/home-standby/home-generator-faq', type: 'Manufacturer', verified: '2026-10-04' },
  ]}
  related={[
    { href: '/cost/standby-generator-quote-checklist/', title: '16-line quote checklist', description: 'Use a blank side-by-side worksheet to normalize competing proposals.', category: 'Cost' },
    { href: '/cost/standby-generator-cost/', title: 'Standby generator cost guide', description: 'Separate manufacturer MSRP from installed-project estimates.', category: 'Cost' },
    { href: '/installation/what-to-expect/', title: 'Installation: what to expect', description: 'See the project stages from site assessment to commissioning and handoff.', category: 'Installation' },
    { href: '/comparisons/five-brand-standby-generator-comparison/', title: 'Five-brand exact-model comparison', description: 'Compare model, fuel, package, warranty, and source scope before requesting quotes.', category: 'Comparisons' },
  ]}
>
  <AlertBox type="info" title="Fictional educational example">The proposal below is invented to show useful scope detail. It is not a real contractor quote, not a price benchmark, and not contract language. Local code, licensing, taxes, utility requirements, consumer-protection rules, and contract terms vary.</AlertBox>

  <section id="sample">
    <h2>Annotated example: the detail you want to see in writing</h2>
    <div class="not-prose mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table sm:min-w-[940px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Fictional annotated standby generator proposal example</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary">
          <tr><th class="p-3">Proposal line</th><th class="p-3">Fictional example wording</th><th class="p-3">Why this is useful</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Generator</th><td class="p-3">“Manufacturer / exact model / 120-240 V single phase / natural-gas rating stated from current manufacturer sheet.”</td><td class="p-3">Prevents a generic kW label from hiding a different fuel rating or substituted model.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Transfer equipment</th><td class="p-3">“Exact ATS model, 200 A, service-entrance rating if applicable; included in proposal.”</td><td class="p-3">Shows that the switch is part of the scope and identifies what is actually being installed.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Backed-up loads</th><td class="p-3">“Proposal is based on attached load schedule. Large loads A/B/C are managed; load D is excluded from generator operation.”</td><td class="p-3">Turns “whole house” into a written operating assumption.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Load management</th><td class="p-3">“Exact module model and quantity; priorities documented during commissioning.”</td><td class="p-3">Avoids discovering later that required control modules were optional extras.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Electrical work</th><td class="p-3">“ATS placement, conductor route, panel/service modifications, trench/penetrations, labeling and restoration described.”</td><td class="p-3">Clarifies whether the total includes the difficult part of the electrical installation.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Fuel work</th><td class="p-3">“Fuel capacity to be verified by named trade/utility. Included work and excluded meter/regulator/tank changes listed separately.”</td><td class="p-3">Assigns responsibility instead of leaving “gas by others” unexplained.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Site / pad</th><td class="p-3">“Proposed location, approved base/pad, grading/drainage, lifting/access and restoration scope stated.”</td><td class="p-3">Makes site preparation visible before construction starts.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Permits</th><td class="p-3">“Electrical/mechanical/fuel permits and inspections: contractor responsible; utility/HOA items separately identified.”</td><td class="p-3">Clarifies who files and who pays.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Commissioning</th><td class="p-3">“Startup, transfer operation test, load-management verification, owner orientation and documentation handoff included.”</td><td class="p-3">Defines completion as more than physically setting the generator in place.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Warranty</th><td class="p-3">“Exact generator and ATS warranty documents linked; registration responsibility and local warranty-service contact named.”</td><td class="p-3">Keeps the warranty attached to the exact equipment instead of a sales slogan.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Allowance</th><td class="p-3">“Example: trench restoration allowance based on stated length/surface; overage requires written change order.”</td><td class="p-3">A defined allowance is easier to evaluate than an open-ended placeholder.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Exclusions</th><td class="p-3">“Landscaping beyond disturbed work area, unforeseen service upgrade, utility meter change, hazardous-material remediation, and other excluded items listed.”</td><td class="p-3">Reduces surprises by stating what the number does not cover.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Commercial terms</th><td class="p-3">“Deposit/payment milestones, taxes, schedule assumptions, cancellation, warranty-service path and written change-order process stated.”</td><td class="p-3">Lets the homeowner compare business terms separately from equipment scope.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="good">
    <h2>What a stronger proposal does</h2>
    <p>A stronger proposal makes the contractor’s assumptions inspectable. It ties the system to exact model numbers, a load plan, a fuel plan, the transfer equipment, site work, permits, and commissioning. It also distinguishes fixed inclusions from allowances and exclusions.</p>
    <p>That does not guarantee a perfect project, and a detailed quote can still contain errors. The value is that disagreements can be traced to written scope rather than reconstructed after work begins.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="red-flags">
    <h2>Ambiguities worth resolving before signing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>“22 kW whole-house package” with no exact generator or ATS model.</li>
      <li>“Gas by others” with no person/company responsible for confirming capacity or completing the connection.</li>
      <li>“Permit as required” with no statement about filing responsibility or fees.</li>
      <li>“Load management included” with no module quantity, model, or controlled loads.</li>
      <li>“Standard installation” with no definition of standard distance, trenching, panel work, or restoration.</li>
      <li>A large “miscellaneous materials” allowance with no basis or reconciliation method.</li>
      <li>Warranty marketing copied into the quote without the actual generator and ATS warranty documents.</li>
      <li>No commissioning / transfer test / owner handoff language.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="change-orders">
    <h2>Allowances and change orders: make uncertainty visible</h2>
    <p>Some site conditions cannot be known until utilities, inspectors, excavation, panel access, or fuel-capacity checks are completed. A responsible proposal can acknowledge that uncertainty. The useful question is whether the allowance has a stated basis and whether the contract explains how any increase or decrease is documented.</p>
    <p>For example, if trench restoration is uncertain, an allowance can identify the assumed length and surface type rather than hiding the uncertainty inside a round number. If a service upgrade is not included, the proposal can state the condition that would trigger separate pricing. Local contract law varies, so use appropriate professional/legal advice for contract terms rather than copying this page as a form.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="before-signing">
    <h2>Before signing, compare the proposal against the 16-line checklist</h2>
    <p>Take each bid and mark generator, ATS, backed-up loads, load management, electrical work, fuel work, site work, permits, commissioning, accessories, warranty, exclusions, allowances, taxes, payment terms and change-order rules. If one proposal remains vague on several categories, ask for clarification in writing before comparing the bottom-line totals.</p>
    <p>Use the <a href="/cost/standby-generator-quote-checklist/">standby generator quote checklist</a> for the blank worksheet and the <a href="/cost/standby-generator-ownership-cost/">5- and 10-year ownership-cost worksheet</a> to keep recurring service/fuel assumptions separate from the installation price.</p>
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
